                <pb facs="0739" n="723" xml:id="pg723"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>At their meetings in New York, September 12–18, the Foreign Ministers of the
                    United States, France, and the United Kingdom, agreed, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >inter alia</hi>, to increase and reinforce their forces in Europe, treat
                    any attack on Berlin or the Federal Republic as an attack on themselves, and
                    “permit the establishment of mobile police formations organized on a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Land</hi> basis but with provisions which would enable
                    the Federal Government to have adequate powers to make effective use of all or
                    part of this force in order to meet the exigencies of the present situation.”
                    For further documentation on the Foreign Ministers meetings, including the text
                    of the communiqué on Germany, see <ref target="frus:frus1950v03/pg_1108">volume
                        III, pages 1108</ref> ff.</p>
            </div>
